{"title":"Complete Quadrilateral","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Complete_quadrilateral\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Complete quadrilateral</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Radical_axis\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Radical axis</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Areas_in_complete_quadrilateral\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Areas in complete quadrilateral</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Newton–Gauss_line\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Newton–Gauss line</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Shatunov-Tokarev_line\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Shatunov-Tokarev line</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Complete_quadrilateral_theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Complete quadrilateral theorem</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Complete_quadrilateral\">Complete quadrilateral</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complete_Quadrilateral&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Complete quadrilateral\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let four lines made four triangles of a complete quadrilateral. In the diagram these are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/5/d15ba44fecfa3229692453358049c41718818e1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC, \\triangle ADE, \\triangle CEF, \\triangle BDF.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"264\" height=\"18\" /> \nOne can see some of the properties of this configuration and their proof using the following links.\n</p>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Miquel%27s_point\" title=\"Miquel&#39;s point\">Miquel's point</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Simson_line\" title=\"Simson line\">Simson line</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Steiner_line\" title=\"Steiner line\">Steiner line</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Gauss_line\" title=\"Gauss line\">Gauss line</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Gauss-Bodenmiller_Theorem\" title=\"Gauss-Bodenmiller Theorem\">Gauss-Bodenmiller Theorem</a></li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Radical_axis\">Radical axis</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complete_Quadrilateral&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Radical axis\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Complete_radical_axes.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/97/Complete_radical_axes.png/400px-Complete_radical_axes.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"400\" height=\"286\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/97/Complete_radical_axes.png/600px-Complete_radical_axes.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/97/Complete_radical_axes.png/800px-Complete_radical_axes.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let four lines made four triangles of a complete quadrilateral. In the diagram these are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/5/d15ba44fecfa3229692453358049c41718818e1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC, \\triangle ADE, \\triangle CEF, \\triangle BDF.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"264\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Let points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/0/f60f22f4712c64c119affdc91fc2f1594e4a4e14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"19\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/a/ecad24a71ff166a321427f872f346a0d4c5f6dd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"28\" height=\"17\" /> be the orthocenters of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/0/1a070d4bf8bcf8e36ef3f368ab964c052a1c2eb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ADE,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"64\" height=\"17\" /> respectively.\n</p><p>Let circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/718eb11a07a0c9778ca69670f33e914d9aad05a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega, \\theta,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/2/9e2b196e9b7e57d1ec99f6534c581ea9759d2170.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Omega$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> be the circles with diameters <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/7/88775bfe5bbf99e99b59e2dfa91678ed500b422a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD, BE,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/a/cba4bccbcf02ccd89a4907b85c1b3a3e02c9a7cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AF,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" /> respectively. \nProve that Steiner line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/6/6365157fc19abb42c8c43de85a9c3d1cf723fcb0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HH_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"15\" /> is the radical axis of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/718eb11a07a0c9778ca69670f33e914d9aad05a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega, \\theta,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/8/de82b5de37f029271b42d807a7a35ac5a7e8e7b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Omega.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><i><b>Proof</b></i>\n</p><p>Let points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/0/140e4392b15f8f48ed8d69c1722b44203dfea4da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G, K, L, N, P,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"109\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> be the foots of perpendiculars <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/5/645f8d90ee718115fb59df9eb5b1e7ee5650caf3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AH_A, CH, DH_A, BH, AH,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"210\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/0/9902dd84cea62eedeedac06e08d2b150b0d11870.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EH_A,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"47\" height=\"18\" /> respectively. \n</p><p>Denote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/4/2d40da2aeded9bfacfd6447011e57d99389d7b19.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Po(X)_{\\omega}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"65\" height=\"20\" /> power of point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> with respect the circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/f/39ff5eebd99794acd0068ae889b1498d54ececd9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega.$\" width=\"15\" height=\"8\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/5/1751f7708cd7afeb2a2eba5cc0fedac7cb644fcb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle AGF = 90^\\circ \\implies G \\in \\Omega \\implies Po(H_A)_{\\Omega} = AH_A \\cdot GH_A.\\]\" width=\"447\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/4/8f42a140addc160d20dd07242fe30414d9f4086f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle APF = 90^\\circ \\implies P \\in \\Omega \\implies Po(H)_{\\Omega} = AH \\cdot PH_A.\\]\" width=\"428\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/d/b6d42cf00a5e05edc26bc604bcf902fa06a9047f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle CLD = 90^\\circ \\implies L \\in \\omega \\implies Po(H_A)_{\\omega} = DH_A \\cdot LH_A = AH_A \\cdot GH_A = Po(H_A)_{\\Omega}.\\]\" width=\"642\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6eedb5b28f2974f98beb1844d2b895f202111a3a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle EQB = 90^\\circ \\implies Q \\in \\theta \\implies Po(H_A)_{\\theta} = EH_A \\cdot QH_A = AH_A \\cdot GH_A = Po(H_A)_{\\Omega}.\\]\" width=\"641\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/c/59c3e66a0e8c6bb2d7283fd28a0b8cc605fb00ed.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle BNE = 90^\\circ \\implies N \\in \\theta \\implies Po(H)_{\\theta} = BH \\cdot NH = AH \\cdot PH = Po(H)_{\\Omega}.\\]\" width=\"592\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/d/5edbb58fd034495eff113e3818b948a53f5f4459.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle CKD = 90^\\circ \\implies K \\in \\theta \\implies Po(H)_{\\omega} = CH \\cdot KH = AH \\cdot PH = Po(H)_{\\Omega}.\\]\" width=\"595\" height=\"18\" />\nTherefore power of point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/9/0c9c037ce393eaad590c9309719c9d1750c76e03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H (H_A)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"58\" height=\"20\" /> with respect these three circles is the same. These points lies on the common radical axis of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/718eb11a07a0c9778ca69670f33e914d9aad05a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega, \\theta,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/4/6f47db04298c9dcc49001627b21728e47871b543.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Omega \\implies$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"14\" /> Steiner line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/6/6365157fc19abb42c8c43de85a9c3d1cf723fcb0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HH_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"15\" /> is the radical axis as desired.\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Areas_in_complete_quadrilateral\">Areas in complete quadrilateral</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complete_Quadrilateral&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Areas in complete quadrilateral\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Complete_areas.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/24/Complete_areas.png/400px-Complete_areas.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"400\" height=\"185\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/24/Complete_areas.png/600px-Complete_areas.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/24/Complete_areas.png/800px-Complete_areas.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let complete quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/2/8029902caf855d940e465d04ff40101d8850c3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCDEF$\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" /> be given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/7/ea750fb647a61166ad4548ad7b8e8677d0257839.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(E = AC \\cap BD, F = AB \\cap CD)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"258\" height=\"20\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/b/d9b020b9801748970d2dd8b9a306f6e959792b1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X, Y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> be the midpoints of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/0/6607d6b7e9771708cb951b01e993a76b7ae2419b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC, AD,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"72\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/3/40396601982527dbcfbddf5f8fa83b1cb3f09ed1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" /> respectively.\n</p><p>Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/9/459dfb080073c655a500b80f453244e0e26123d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {[ADFE]}{[ADC]} = \\frac {ZY}{XY},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"138\" height=\"45\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/8/8a86702dc60b9db916798b1115a6b6822bf828b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[t]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"14\" height=\"18\" /> is the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/9/519c6c8c4f585649e734eab8902feb0602d8c5d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t.$\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" />\n</p><p><i><b>Proof</b></i>\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Complete_areas_1.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/1/14/Complete_areas_1.png/400px-Complete_areas_1.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"400\" height=\"245\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/1/14/Complete_areas_1.png/600px-Complete_areas_1.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/1/14/Complete_areas_1.png/800px-Complete_areas_1.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/6/a568aec5e9b4a96863541c3b03490066103aa2dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X_1, Y_1, D_1, C_1, Z_1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"143\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/2/3a2731abbb0e2524327bba81e355d6d886e5a6cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" /> be the projections of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/4/4f437ae238083ca376cbbe062c232f93f3e08a85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X, Y, A, B, Z,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"107\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/a/a0a1dbdebe8f3df1befc34f5862f69364b3c11b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"18\" /> respectively onto <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/c/79c918bfa9b7b699552d7779d1de346b73d75010.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD.$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/718eb11a07a0c9778ca69670f33e914d9aad05a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega, \\theta,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/2/9e2b196e9b7e57d1ec99f6534c581ea9759d2170.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Omega$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> be the circles with diameters <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/9/0c936adb0bf2cf3d6a6e231ff0ea893d52415ea1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD, BC,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"69\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/3/40396601982527dbcfbddf5f8fa83b1cb3f09ed1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" /> respectively. \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/8/d2837b3217b03b2d197da6858538225fcd0090a9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle EF_1F = 90^\\circ \\implies F_1 \\in \\Omega.\\]\" width=\"217\" height=\"16\" />\nSimilarly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/9/529ca9b6527694bc039d094ec9e0899eb28e8c2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_1 \\in \\omega, C_1 \\in \\theta.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"123\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Let common radical axes of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/4/d84983371096f1c75816a9d2421eb492999018a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega, \\Omega,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cross <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/0/4e037912024535666d4f5194dcf67a6231061c8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G.$\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/1/4611ccbdb8dcb797cf81e8ff661fb3ed5de6032e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[EF_1 || AD_1 \\implies \\frac {EC}{AC} = \\frac {F_1C}{D_1C}.\\]\" width=\"230\" height=\"40\" />\nThe power of the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> with respect <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/718eb11a07a0c9778ca69670f33e914d9aad05a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega, \\theta,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/2/9e2b196e9b7e57d1ec99f6534c581ea9759d2170.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Omega$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> is the same, therefore\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/1/1416a9239188b153a70685e855b8fb082fb82375.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[GF \\cdot GF_1 = GC \\cdot GC_1 = GD \\cdot GD_1 \\implies\\]\" width=\"318\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/1/b513902982de862729ff585e2e3dec1474d5dbd1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(\\vec G - \\vec F) \\cdot (\\vec G - \\vec F_1) = (\\vec G - \\vec C) \\cdot (\\vec G - \\vec C_1) = (\\vec G - \\vec D) \\cdot (\\vec G – \\vec D_1) \\implies\\]\" width=\"472\" height=\"22\" /> \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/0/87047dad0c32a071c829bc934583f91b95f8537d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\vec F \\cdot \\vec F_1 – \\vec G \\cdot (\\vec F + \\vec F_1) = \\vec F \\cdot \\vec F_1 – \\vec G \\cdot 2\\vec Z_1 = \\vec C \\cdot \\vec C_1 – \\vec G \\cdot (\\vec C + \\vec C_1) =\\]\" width=\"479\" height=\"22\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/e/eee70aa2002d46619720d3b4bee535eb13ab387c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[= \\vec C \\cdot \\vec C_1 - \\vec G \\cdot 2\\vec X_1 = \\vec D \\cdot \\vec D_1 – \\vec G \\cdot (\\vec D + \\vec D_1) = \\vec D \\cdot \\vec D_1 - \\vec G \\cdot 2\\vec Y_1 \\implies\\]\" width=\"473\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/6/726080ec6f893b4dc3307cf9c8af2433b310f187.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[|\\vec G| = \\frac {\\vec F \\cdot\\vec  F_1 - \\vec C \\cdot \\vec C_1}{2|\\vec Z_1 - \\vec X_1|} = \\frac {\\vec D \\cdot \\vec D_1 - \\vec C \\cdot \\vec C_1}{2|\\vec Y_1 – \\vec X_1|} \\implies\\]\" width=\"355\" height=\"50\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/d/76dec48af04d87fd1e562c6ca64ca179f3b24320.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac {|\\vec Z_1 - \\vec X_1|}{|\\vec Y_1 - \\vec X_1|} =  \\frac {\\vec F \\cdot \\vec F_1 - \\vec C \\cdot \\vec C_1} {\\vec D \\cdot \\vec D_1 - \\vec C \\cdot\\vec C_1} = \\frac {Z_1X_1}{Y_1X_1} = \\frac {ZX}{YX} .\\]\" width=\"359\" height=\"50\" />\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/c/84c092cb77aac45c367437fa81fbbe8760ef7fdd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec C = \\vec 0 \\implies \\vec C \\cdot\\vec C_1 = 0, \\vec F \\cdot \\vec F_1 = FC \\cdot F_1C, \\vec D \\cdot \\vec D_1 = DC \\cdot D_1C.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"500\" height=\"23\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/8/cd8ac6ad83eebccd4b2607faf12d390bf278b14b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac {ZX}{YX} = \\frac {FC \\cdot F_1C} {DC \\cdot D_1C} = \\frac {FC \\cdot EC} {DC \\cdot AC} = \\frac {[CEF]}{[CAD]}.\\]\" width=\"325\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Therefore  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/2/5c23d8448404eb06507acec4ee9e9f81a9f92395.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac {[ADEF]}{[CAD]} =  \\frac {[CEF]-[CAD]}{[CAD]} =\\frac {ZX - YX}{YX} = \\frac {ZY}{YX}.\\]\" width=\"401\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Newton.E2.80.93Gauss_line\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Newton–Gauss_line\">Newton–Gauss line</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complete_Quadrilateral&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Newton–Gauss line\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Complete_perpendicular.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/25/Complete_perpendicular.png/500px-Complete_perpendicular.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"500\" height=\"300\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/25/Complete_perpendicular.png/750px-Complete_perpendicular.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/25/Complete_perpendicular.png/1000px-Complete_perpendicular.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let four lines made four triangles of a complete quadrilateral.\n</p><p>In the diagram these are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/5/d15ba44fecfa3229692453358049c41718818e1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC, \\triangle ADE, \\triangle CEF, \\triangle BDF.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"264\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Let points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/5/2058fb2fac59b6c8536586e65d1a83554090676f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K, L,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> be the midpoints of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/d/a6df8c8faa752f025d5126f18e8e82ab016a44a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BE, CD,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/a/cba4bccbcf02ccd89a4907b85c1b3a3e02c9a7cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AF,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" /> respectively.\n</p><p>Let points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/a/ecad24a71ff166a321427f872f346a0d4c5f6dd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"28\" height=\"17\" /> be the orthocenters of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/0/1a070d4bf8bcf8e36ef3f368ab964c052a1c2eb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ADE,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"64\" height=\"17\" /> respectively.\n</p><p>Prove that Steiner line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/6/6365157fc19abb42c8c43de85a9c3d1cf723fcb0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HH_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"15\" /> is perpendicular to Gauss line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/1/0c19f4900825ee30b43b6f1e3e9b52e866745035.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KLN.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><i><b>Proof</b></i>\n</p><p>Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/5/2058fb2fac59b6c8536586e65d1a83554090676f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K, L,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> are the centers of circles with diameters  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/d/a6df8c8faa752f025d5126f18e8e82ab016a44a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BE, CD,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/a/cba4bccbcf02ccd89a4907b85c1b3a3e02c9a7cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AF,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" /> respectively.\n</p><p>Steiner line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/6/6365157fc19abb42c8c43de85a9c3d1cf723fcb0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HH_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"15\" /> is the radical axis of these circles.\n</p><p>Therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/9/549e2ba66c5308a0d1fb76c0607581de9a88a2cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HH_A \\perp KL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"17\" /> as desired.\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Shatunov-Tokarev_line\">Shatunov-Tokarev line</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complete_Quadrilateral&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Shatunov-Tokarev line\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Shatunov_line_3.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/7/7a/Shatunov_line_3.png/500px-Shatunov_line_3.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"500\" height=\"332\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/7/7a/Shatunov_line_3.png/750px-Shatunov_line_3.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/7/7a/Shatunov_line_3.png/1000px-Shatunov_line_3.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let the complete quadrilateral ABCDEF be labeled as in the diagram. Quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/c/91c493dd2882ca1eb621281106a4d91bd73e092e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCED$\" width=\"58\" height=\"12\" /> is not cyclic.\n</p><p>Let points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/e/afe6313d6be448f2c4e4382a0356268847df67fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H, H_A, H_B, H_C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"120\" height=\"18\" /> be the orthocenters and points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/4/df46034e705bab7cf00d2c036b5d4851909e3d67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O, O_A, O_B, O_C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"18\" /> be the circumcenters of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/d/65d67d7b126e182494956777e6f3bf208983f713.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC, \\triangle ADE, \\triangle BDF,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"199\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/1/661a39ce1626acc26216acc56960bf2038e285f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle CEF,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" /> respectively.\n</p><p>Let bisector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> cross bisector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/b/26b727b773d199f4fa0d684957a720f9c3e9750c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"16\" /> Let bisector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> cross bisector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/5/6d5c9afa92ae3180f02754c177e530d9d0f8b4d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/c/e3ce33ec2a34dc4fddaec3224043f4a72bbe6d0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Prove that \n</p><p>a) points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> lie on circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/5/3c5a338f93675fd4865187d9bc4ea84e689a7a79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle OO_AO_C,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" /> \n</p><p>b) line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/b/7ab17967324fc2b12349c0610c6c256f4c829162.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> is symmetric to Steiner line with respect centroid of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/2/80241df278acc6c33f96f855ab0e5e24d54f411b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BDEC.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"65\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>I suppose that this line was found independently by two young mathematicians Leonid Shatunov and Alexander Tokarev in 2022. I would be grateful for information on whether this line was previously known.\n</p><p><i><b>Proof</b></i>\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Shatunov_line_2.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/6/68/Shatunov_line_2.png/500px-Shatunov_line_2.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"500\" height=\"385\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/6/68/Shatunov_line_2.png/750px-Shatunov_line_2.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/6/68/Shatunov_line_2.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>a) Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lies on bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/1/ce1f5a2ad34bb6fb5169f99e437b9f3e21d1887e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" /> points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/8/4d8aa423de2031c09aa06a1687767414dbb8120d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" /> lies on bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/c/f7c6268d54c1d363f43d512f2bb17356ec295749.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DE \\implies$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"68\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/0/9f0139460773d4bc960222d72f8f24fcc720befc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle OPO_A + \\angle OO_CO_A = 180^\\circ \\implies P \\in$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"307\" height=\"18\" /> circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/6/fa6257e5e8adc8c1e019842f1ae9bdf763aa1637.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OO_AO_C.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"71\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Similarly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/6/4d6cd7768463ad13bc25beaeedba16b2c435dedc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P \\in$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"13\" /> circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/c/2fc3b98c1f50312a6d1ff7b3d82ed57029ca454f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OO_BO_C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"17\" /> as desired.\n</p>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Miquel%27s_point\" title=\"Miquel&#39;s point\">Miquel's point</a></li></ul>\n<p>b) Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/8/3f8b5dfe32fa1f95bff0104a99283397c6220789.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M, M&#39;, P&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"80\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be midpoints of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/8/a4843f57bb89a5de43a52e18dec452c178bc1277.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE, BD, HH_A,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"125\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/e/ade444b0dbd3ca7f2b35425561253a22b2217cf8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MM&#39;,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"19\" /> respectively.\n</p><p>It is clear that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is centroid of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/2/80241df278acc6c33f96f855ab0e5e24d54f411b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BDEC.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"65\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/0/6e0ad5acdd6e8c6363c66d6d64b688809667897f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M&#39;P \\perp BD, EH_A \\perp AD, CH \\perp AB \\implies MP&#39; \\perp AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"429\" height=\"19\" /> (midline of trapezium <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/7/bb7634cca367d42c8d8dae3ad4dc69c4c7798132.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ECHH_A) \\implies EH_A||MP&#39;||CH||M&#39;P.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"311\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/4/2344f21f613935620a0feb3b2ede64984c11f1e8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[MP \\perp CE, DH_A \\perp AE, BH \\perp AC \\implies\\]\" width=\"318\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/4/9f491f302fccddda39a88e17b75756793a5e328e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M&#39;P&#39; \\perp AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"98\" height=\"16\" /> (midline of trapezium <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/a/26ac56ac397d89662319f4f5d9b7c2b853aa5cfc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HBDH_A) \\implies$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"126\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/3/af3d2b1b505f5d8d1e9108355e1c254eff3fc0ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DH_A||M&#39;P&#39;||BH||MP \\implies M&#39;P&#39;MP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"308\" height=\"20\" /> is parallelogram.\n</p><p>Similarly one can prove that point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/1/ad1fc259525ad84b501cf1c525fc33664af827a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q&#39;,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"19\" /> the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/e/d3e478d03a51794265d6d25026ee7e24fb7cdf43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H_BH_C,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> is symmetric to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> with respect <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/0/4e037912024535666d4f5194dcf67a6231061c8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G.$\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p>Therefore line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/2/58235ee706438b477de873005a8a7dec97ebb377.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P&#39;Q&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"17\" /> coincide with Steiner line and line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/b/7ab17967324fc2b12349c0610c6c256f4c829162.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> is symmetric to Steiner line with respect <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and is parallel to this line.\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Complete_quadrilateral_theorem\">Complete quadrilateral theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complete_Quadrilateral&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Complete quadrilateral theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Complete_quadrilateral_map.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/d/db/Complete_quadrilateral_map.png/350px-Complete_quadrilateral_map.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"350\" height=\"401\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/d/db/Complete_quadrilateral_map.png/525px-Complete_quadrilateral_map.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/db/Complete_quadrilateral_map.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/6521ad84932f05bbfa1185b24d793813c7aefc2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C, D,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"85\" height=\"16\" /> no three of which are collinear, be given.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/5/2e5c4f4105d797ef07c0c84347236efe57c59ca3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P = AD \\cap BC, Q = AC \\cap BD, E = AB \\cap PQ, F = CD \\cap PQ.\\]\" width=\"493\" height=\"16\" />\nProve that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/d/46df7ef78b9bd37bddad7912caf04723bd0cb850.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{QE \\cdot PF}{PE \\cdot QF} = 1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"115\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p><i><b>Proof</b></i>\n</p><p>We make the projective transformation of the vertices of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> into vertices of the square. (<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Projective_geometry_(simplest_cases)\" title=\"Projective geometry (simplest cases)\">Projecting non-convex quadrilateral into rectangle</a>) \nThen image of the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the point at infinity, image of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> is the center of the square, images of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/b/6bb8deb6bd62b989e41c20df300bde9bb41e9c81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD, PQ,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> are parallel, so for images <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/f/acf8f640adfc2218aa11d4a48c63abfb588b6b58.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$QF = QE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/3/7c37788e6c6205cdaa46bd17d7d55867c867c238.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac {PE}{PF} = 1  \\implies \\frac{QE \\cdot PF}{PE \\cdot QF} = 1.\\]\" width=\"224\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p>The double ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/6/0e68fecd45e2f7bb9893bbddc2232f383d7798c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{QE \\cdot PF}{PE \\cdot QF}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"76\" height=\"43\" /> is the projective invariant of a quadruple of collinear points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/b/53b70bc37ac958176187d63c56c4af5b6aa304d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(P,Q;E,F)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"95\" height=\"20\" /> so the equality also holds for the preimages.\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017201645\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.076 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.217 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 710/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 26848/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:23496:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017201645 and revision id 254929. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}